<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What today is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">The move that makes this class different from most.</p>
  <p>AI outputs are sources. Same OPVL framework, same critical eye. Today we make that move explicit by running OPVL on an <strong>AI summary of the same paper you OPVL'd last class</strong>: <a href="/units/unit-0/block-2/">Lovelock (1965)</a>. Same content, different source type. The Origin / Purpose / Value / Limitation outputs will diverge. The differences between those two OPVLs are the lesson. Then we introduce the habit you'll carry for the rest of the year: <strong>every time you use AI in your work, you fill out the AI Documentation Template</strong>.</p>
  <p>The documentation isn't there to police you. It's there to make you notice what the AI did versus what you did. Students who can articulate that distinction learn more from AI than students who can't. Honest disclosure = no penalty. Hidden AI use = academic integrity violation. That's the whole protocol. Want to see the protocol working on a real document? The <a href="/units/unit-0/exemplar-source-dossier/#ai-doc">exemplar dossier's own AI Documentation page</a> is a live example: the exemplar was built with AI and documents itself the same way you will.</p>
  <p>One more thing, said plainly: on our class AI platform, Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt can see student conversations. That's not surveillance of your thinking; it's the same principle as the Documentation Template. In this class, AI use is visible by design. Work as if your process will be read, because it will be.</p>
</section>

