<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work</h2>
  <p class="sub">One task today. Everyone runs the same comparison, because the whole point is that we can put our answers side by side.</p>
  <div class="choice-intro">
    <strong>Learning Intention:</strong> {{ learningIntention | safe }}
  </div>
  <div class="callout task">
    <div class="callout-title">Everyone does this</div>
    <p>Ask the class AI platform to summarise <strong>Lovelock (1965)</strong>. Save the conversation. Then OPVL the AI's response, and fill in the full AI Documentation Template. Last, put it beside your own OPVL from Block 2: what specifically is different in the Limitation line? That difference is today's lesson.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout extend">
    <div class="callout-title">If you finish early</div>
    <p>Open a second, fresh conversation and give it the identical prompt. Put the two answers side by side. Are they the same? Where they differ, does the difference change what you would trust? Two runs of one prompt disagreeing is itself a Limitation, and it is worth naming in your template.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">If you are stuck</div>
    <p>Work with a partner and open the sample AI Documentation entry from Google Classroom. It has some fields done well and some done badly. Use it as the model for what "specific" looks like, then come back to your own.</p>
  </div>
